REPLAY (bit-for-bit; case N depends only on the seed and on N)
  ./fuzz/run.sh --seed 12648430 --cases 2000

  Or drive the binary directly, from the repo root:
    ./fuzz/differential/target/release/difffuzz \
        --seed 12648430 --cases 2000 --batch 2000 --start 0 \
        --oracle C:/Users/pc/Desktop/portmortem-2026/fuzz/build/oracle_harness_sabotaged.exe --workdir fuzz/build/work --log fuzz/log.txt

  A single case can be re-run on its own:
    ... --seed 12648430 --case <INDEX>

  Batch size does not affect the stream: inputs are derived per case as
  SplitMix64(seed ^ SplitMix64(index)), so any --batch replays the same bytes.

  That is checkable rather than merely asserted. Re-run the seed at a DIFFERENT
  batch size and the input stream digest above must come out identical:
    ./fuzz/run.sh --seed 12648430 --cases 2000 --batch 7919
    -> input stream digest must be 0xE7ECDA4FF11C3950
  This run was time-bounded (--seconds 6); the replay above is case-bounded
  at the count actually reached, which reproduces it exactly.

HOW THE TWO SIDES ARE COMPARED
  Shared public API. Both sides are handed identical bytes and asked for all 13
  exported entry points, plus the same twelve values rebuilt one byte at a time
  through the 8 update_crc_* functions.

  Oracle   the ORIGINAL C libcrc, built from the pristine upstream tree with the
           project's own CFLAGS (-O3 -funsigned-char). It lives in the gitignored
           oracle/ directory and nothing in crates/ links, calls or depends on it.
  Port     crates/libcrc-rs, called directly as a Rust library. No FFI, no shim,
           no C in the measured path.

  BATCH, NOT STREAM. Each round writes a whole batch to a file, runs the oracle
  once with stdin closed, waits for it to exit, then reads the results file. No
  pipe is ever read, so the Windows anonymous-pipe deadlock that a long-lived
  bidirectional oracle would risk has no surface here. CRC is a pure function;
  there is nothing to interleave.

