  null-pointer                            4    0.20%

  The 'fixed-corpus' class is a deterministic prologue that runs before any
  random input, so these are covered on EVERY run rather than probabilistically:
    - empty input
    - all 256 byte values as a single byte, and as a 17-byte uniform fill
    - lengths 1,7,8,9,15,16,17,31,32,33,63,64,65,127,128,129 under four fill
      patterns (zero, 0xFF, counting, 0x55/0xAA)
    - all-zero and all-0xFF buffers at 0,1,255,256,257,1023,1024,1025,4095,4096,4097
    - long buffers: 65535, 65536, 65537 and 262144 bytes, four fill patterns
    - NMEA sentences, with and without the leading '$', terminated by end-of-string,
      '*', CR, LF and CRLF, including embedded NULs and delimiters at interior
      offsets
    - the NULL-pointer contract at four lengths (libcrc returns the init value from
      its twelve CRC functions and NULL from checksum_NMEA)

ORACLE SELF-CHECK (run before the clock started)
  The oracle is asked for "123456789" and its answers are compared against the
  values recorded from the upstream library in 00-VERIFIED-FACTS.md §11. If the
  oracle were mis-built — most likely by dropping -funsigned-char, which libcrc
  requires — it would be wrong itself and every 'divergence' below would be an
  artefact. Result: PASS

